    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/flushable-containers.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Liveness gates the prune, and \"cannot tell\" spares the item",
      "prov:value": "An item can look dead and be live. Measured 2026-09-19 (bean `w2g5`): the\nstaging orphan check called a preview abandoned because its branch had no open\nPR, when the branch had been committed to **two minutes earlier** by a session\nthat reuses one branch across a run of PRs. \"No open PR\" was read as a synonym\nfor \"abandoned\" and it is not.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean w2g5",
        "2026-09-19"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/flushable-containers.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A limit below the floor is not a limit, it is a permanent alarm",
      "prov:value": "**Measured 2026-09-19, and this is the finding that motivated the skill.**\n`STAGING_WARN_BYTES` is 100 MB, on the owner's instruction and a tenth of\nGitHub's 1 GB Pages budget. A single preview is **~38 MB**, so the limit holds\n**2.6 previews**. On that day the publish branch carried **9** previews totalling\n**346 MB**, of which — by the liveness rule above — exactly **2** were prunable,\nfreeing 76.7 MB and leaving **269 MB**, still 2.7× the limit.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-19"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/flushable-containers.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "A limit below the floor is not a limit, it is a permanent alarm",
      "prov:value": "Seven live previews × 38 MB is a **266 MB floor** that no amount of pruning can\ngo under while those sessions are working. A limit beneath its own floor cannot\nbe satisfied, so it fires constantly — and a signal that always fires is the\n`xom7` defect, where a workflow failed 30 times and the failure became invisible\n*because* it was constant."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/flushable-containers.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A limit below the floor is not a limit, it is a permanent alarm",
      "prov:value": "**But \"make the references relative\" is not the fix, and the owner caught this.**\nA reference is either an **address** or a **name**, and only addresses may be\nrelativized. A stylesheet href is an address; a JSON-LD `@id` or a JSON Schema\n`$ref` is a name, and a relative one resolves against the *retrieval* URL — so\nthe graph's identities become a function of how the file was fetched, and an\nout-of-band validator has no base at all. Deduplication is worth having; it is\nnot worth buying with identity. The 5205 identity references in that corpus were\nleft byte-for-byte alone, which is why the projection landed at ~77 MB rather\nthan one copy: **the exports are a real floor, not a duplication artefact.**"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/flushable-containers.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "A limit below the floor is not a limit, it is a permanent alarm",
      "prov:value": "That is the rule's second application in one investigation. The first \"prune\nmore\" was hiding a 9× duplication; a second one would hide a genuine floor —\nand a genuine floor is answered by a limit whose **basis states it**, never by a\npolicy. Worked out in bean `xxku`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean xxku"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/flushable-containers.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Not built yet",
      "prov:value": "The **viewer** — a counter, an over-full badge and a select dialog — is bean\n`7vhe`, deliberately separate: it is UI with different constraints, and `gjli`\nmakes accessibility a standing rule, which a select dialog is the control most\noften shipped without. The **staging** mechanism is its own work (`w2g5`): a\npreflight plus a dispatchable cleanup path, because the label-based remedy was\nunreachable for exactly the previews the check can find. This skill is the\nconcept those two implement, not a third implementation of it.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean w2g5"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
